 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 as a real-time radiance field representation. Subsequent research identified limitations in its ability to represent complex geometry, handle defocus, and reconstruct watertight surfaces. Negative Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2] and Scaffold-GS [S1] address representation and initialization issues. DARBF-Splatting [S1] explores alternative kernels for efficiency. Gaussian Wrapping [S2] adapts the primitives for surface reconstruction. Gaussian Shell Maps [S1] integrate CNNs for human avatars. These methods collectively expand the applicability of 3DGS to more challenging scenes and tasks.
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [G]. The standard representation uses positive Gaussians with spherical harmonics for color. Subsequent research identified limitations in handling complex shapes, sparse initialization, and geometric consistency. Negative Gaussian Splatting [S1, S2] and Scaffold-GS [S1] address representation flexibility and initialization sparsity. GeoGaussian [S1] and Gaussian Wrapping [S2] focus on geometric priors and surface reconstruction. DARBF-Splatting [S1] explores alternative kernel functions for efficiency. Grid-based blur kernels [S1] address defocus modeling. These methods collectively expand the representational capacity of 3DGS beyond the original exponential Gaussian kernel.
 
